# Compute Flow Application SDK

This directory contains the public headers, libraries, and installed brick metadata produced by the
corresponding `compute-flow build <target>` build.

Metadata for the installed bricks included in the build is available under
`share/compute_flow/bricks/<type>/<name>/info.json`. Applications can use each declared model's
ordered sources to download it and its `sha256` field to validate the downloaded `SHA256SUMS` file.
This means applications do not need to maintain a separate registry of model URLs and checksums for
the installed bricks.

Add the SDK to an application and link its convenience target:

```cmake
add_subdirectory("/path/to/sdk" compute_flow_sdk)
target_link_libraries(my_application PRIVATE compute_flow::sdk)
```

The `compute_flow::sdk` target also configures the application to compile with C++17 or later and
supplies the platform-specific link requirements of the packaged libraries.

Applications using the SDK on Android™ must configure their final executables and shared libraries
for 16 KB page sizes. NDK r28 and later provide compatible alignment by default. With NDK r27 or
earlier, add the alignment options to the application's top-level `CMakeLists.txt` after
`project()`:

```cmake
if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "Android")
    foreach(linker_flags IN ITEMS CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS CMAKE_SHARED_LINKER_FLAGS CMAKE_MODULE_LINKER_FLAGS)
        string(APPEND ${linker_flags} " -Wl,-z,max-page-size=16384 -Wl,-z,common-page-size=16384")
    endforeach()
endif()
```

See the [Android™ 16 KB page-size guide](https://developer.android.com/guide/practices/page-sizes)
for compatibility requirements and verification steps.

To use a copied SDK directory as an application, add `main.cpp` and configure the directory as the
top-level CMake project. `PROJ_NAME` defaults to `minimal_example`; change it in `CMakeLists.txt` to
use another executable name. The provided CMake file creates the executable and links it to
`compute_flow::sdk` automatically.

The packaged libraries reflect the bricks and build options selected when Compute Flow was
configured. Installed implementations are exposed through generic brick-type libraries such as
`libtts_core.a`; concrete variant archives are not included. Rebuild the SDK after changing the
installed bricks or target configuration.
